<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-verifying-solutions-systems" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Verifying Solutions To a System</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Back in the very first chapter, before we could solve ODEs, we learned how to
    <em>verify</em> that a given function was a solution: plug it in and check that both sides
    match. We'll take the same first step with systems. Given a vector of functions, how do we
    check whether it solves the matrix equation
    <m>\frac{d\mathbf{x}}{dt} = \mathbf{P}(t)\mathbf{x} + \mathbf{f}(t)</m>?
    As before, we just compute both sides and compare!
  </p>

  <p>
    One note on notation regarding the next example: we'll name our candidate solutions
    <m>\mathbf{y}</m> and <m>\mathbf{z}</m> so their names are distinct from the component
    functions <m>x_1</m> and <m>x_2</m>.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXAMPLE: THREE PARTS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-verify-solutions-system">
    <title>Verifying Solutions</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the system:
        <md>
          <mrow>x_1' \amp= 4x_1 - 3x_2</mrow>
          <mrow>x_2' \amp= 6x_1 - 7x_2</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write the system as a matrix equation.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Every coefficient is present here (no missing terms!), and there are no leftover
          terms, so <m>\mathbf{f}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>:
          <me>\frac{d\mathbf{x}}{dt}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp -3 \\ 6 \amp -7 \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \end{bmatrix}
            + \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.</me>
          Since <m>\mathbf{f}(t)</m> is the zero vector, we usually just drop it and write
          <me>\frac{d\mathbf{x}}{dt}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp -3 \\ 6 \amp -7 \end{bmatrix} \mathbf{x}.</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Verify that
          <m>\mathbf{y}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} 3e^{2t} \\ 2e^{2t} \end{bmatrix}</m>
          is a solution.</p>
          
          <p> Let's do this one together. We need to check that
          <m>\dfrac{d\mathbf{y}}{dt} = \mathbf{P}\mathbf{y}</m>, so we compute each side
          separately.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the left-hand side, we differentiate each component:
          <me>\frac{d\mathbf{y}}{dt}
            = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{d}{dt}\left(3e^{2t}\right) \\ \frac{d}{dt}\left(2e^{2t}\right) \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 6e^{2t} \\ 4e^{2t} \end{bmatrix}.</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          For the right-hand side, we multiply:
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathbf{P}\mathbf{y}
              = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp -3 \\ 6 \amp -7 \end{bmatrix}
              \begin{bmatrix} 3e^{2t} \\ 2e^{2t} \end{bmatrix}
              \amp= \begin{bmatrix} 4 \cdot 3e^{2t} - 3 \cdot 2e^{2t} \\ 6 \cdot 3e^{2t} - 7 \cdot 2e^{2t} \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= \begin{bmatrix} (12 - 6)e^{2t} \\ (18 - 14)e^{2t} \end{bmatrix}
              = \begin{bmatrix} 6e^{2t} \\ 4e^{2t} \end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
          </md>
          The two sides match, so <m>\mathbf{y}(t)</m> is a solution!
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now it's your turn: verify that
          <m>\mathbf{z}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} e^{-5t} \\ 3e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix}</m>
          is also a solution. Work it out on paper before checking the solution below,
          and then use the checkpoint question that follows to confirm your computation.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Follow the same two steps: differentiate each component of <m>\mathbf{z}</m> to get
          <m>\frac{d\mathbf{z}}{dt}</m> (don't forget the chain rule!), then compute
          <m>\mathbf{P}\mathbf{z}</m> with matrix-vector multiplication. You're hoping the
          two results agree.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Differentiating (chain rule brings down a <m>-5</m>):
          <me>\frac{d\mathbf{z}}{dt}
            = \begin{bmatrix} -5e^{-5t} \\ -15e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix}.</me>
          Multiplying:
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathbf{P}\mathbf{z}
              = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp -3 \\ 6 \amp -7 \end{bmatrix}
              \begin{bmatrix} e^{-5t} \\ 3e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix}
              \amp= \begin{bmatrix} 4 \cdot e^{-5t} - 3 \cdot 3e^{-5t} \\ 6 \cdot e^{-5t} - 7 \cdot 3e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= \begin{bmatrix} (4-9)e^{-5t} \\ (6-21)e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix}
              = \begin{bmatrix} -5e^{-5t} \\ -15e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
          </md>
          Both sides equal
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} -5e^{-5t} \\ -15e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          so <m>\mathbf{z}(t)</m> is a solution as well.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACTIVITY: CHECKPOINT ON PART (c) (MC)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <activity xml:id="act-verify-z-checkpoint">
    <title>Checking Your Verification</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        When verifying <m>\mathbf{z}(t)</m> in the last part, you computed both
        <m>\dfrac{d\mathbf{z}}{dt}</m> and <m>\mathbf{P}\mathbf{z}</m>. What vector did you
        get (for both)?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\begin{bmatrix} -5e^{-5t} \\ -15e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! The chain rule brings a factor of <m>-5</m> out of each component of
            <m>\mathbf{z}</m>, and the matrix product gives <m>(4-9)e^{-5t} = -5e^{-5t}</m>
            and <m>(6-21)e^{-5t} = -15e^{-5t}</m>. The two sides agree, so
            <m>\mathbf{z}(t)</m> is a solution.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\begin{bmatrix} 5e^{-5t} \\ 15e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Check your signs! Differentiating <m>e^{-5t}</m> brings down a factor of
            <m>-5</m>, not <m>5</m>. And on the matrix side, <m>4 - 9 = -5</m> and
            <m>6 - 21 = -15</m>: both negative.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\begin{bmatrix} -e^{-5t} \\ -3e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This is just <m>-\mathbf{z}(t)</m>, which suggests the chain rule got skipped.
            Differentiating <m>e^{-5t}</m> brings down the full factor of <m>-5</m>:
            <m>\frac{d}{dt}e^{-5t} = -5e^{-5t}</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The two sides did not match, so <m>\mathbf{z}(t)</m> is not a solution.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            They should match! Recompute carefully: the derivative side gives
            <m>-5e^{-5t}</m> and <m>-15e^{-5t}</m>, and the matrix side gives
            <m>(4-9)e^{-5t}</m> and <m>(6-21)e^{-5t}</m>, which are the same thing.
            A stray sign or a slipped chain rule is the usual culprit here.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </activity>

  <p>
    We just found two different solutions to the same system. There are actually
    infinitely many more! Just like with second-order equations back in
    <xref ref="ch-second-order"/>, we'll soon see how to combine solutions to build the
    general solution.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="takeaways-verifying-solutions">
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            To verify that a vector of functions solves
            <m>\dfrac{d\mathbf{x}}{dt} = \mathbf{P}(t)\mathbf{x}</m>, compute both sides
            separately and check that they give the same vector.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The left-hand side is computed by differentiating each component
            (watch the chain rule!), and the right-hand side by matrix-vector multiplication.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A system can have several different solutions. Soon we will see how to combine
            them into the general solution.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

